'website-performance-optimization'=>[
"Website Performance Optimization: A Practical Guide for Businesses","Web Performance","A fast website helps users reach information with less friction. Performance work should be based on measurement rather than guesswork and should consider images, CSS, JavaScript, caching, hosting and third-party scripts.",[
["Measure before changing the site","Use performance tools and real-user data to identify the slowest or least stable parts of the experience. Measurement prevents teams from spending time on changes that have little impact."],
["Optimize images and fonts","Large images and inefficient font loading are common sources of page weight. Use appropriate formats, responsive image sizes, lazy loading where suitable and a limited set of font weights."],
["Reduce unnecessary JavaScript","Review third-party scripts, unused libraries and client-side work. Many pages can become faster by sending less code to the browser and delaying non-essential functionality."],
["Use caching and compression","Browser caching, server-side caching and compression can reduce repeat-load costs. Configuration should be tested so dynamic content is not cached incorrectly."],
["Monitor real-user performance","Lab tests are useful, but real-user measurements show how devices, networks and locations affect the experience. Monitor trends over time so regressions are visible."],
]],
'crm-erp-integration-guide'=>[
"How CRM and ERP Integration Can Improve Business Workflows","Business Systems","Connecting CRM and ERP systems can reduce duplicate data entry and give teams a more consistent view of customers, orders, inventory and operations. Integration should be designed around clear data ownership.",[
["Map the systems and data owners","Start with a map of CRM, ERP, finance, inventory, support and other systems. For each data type, identify which system is the source of truth and which systems consume it."],
["Define synchronization rules","Specify which records move between systems, when they move and what happens when data conflicts. Clear rules reduce duplicate records and unpredictable updates."],
["Protect sensitive information","Use authentication, authorization, encryption and appropriate data minimization. Integration credentials should be protected and access should be limited to what each service needs."],
["Handle failures and retries","Networks fail and third-party services can become unavailable. Integrations should handle retries, timeouts, duplicate events and partial failures without silently corrupting business data."],
["Monitor the integration after launch","Logging and alerts make it easier to identify failed jobs or unexpected data patterns. Integration monitoring is part of the operational product, not just a development concern."],
]],
